 you kind of just  see that the group  naturally starts to create learning for  each other  um and then at the end of the class  there's a  weekend or a couple days where you do  really just  that but like for eight or 12 hours at a  time  and then you do a lot of reflection and  a lot of writing and  i think it's a really great course um  if that's a focus of yours um and then  one other class that i would add in the  soft skills  area is um it was a communications class  i don't remember the name of it but  really you're just learning how to  communicate and you have to do  a lot of presentation a lot of different  exercises and i think it sounds like it  should be straightforward  but it you actually take a lot out of it  if communication is something you want  to work on  and i believe the instructor i can't  remember her name but  is someone who's very well known and  the communication coaching world has  been working with stanford students  for a long long time and i just found  her  what she'd pick up about the nuances of  your  not just your verbal uh your oral  communication but your  non-verbal communication to be really  helpful  so we have a great question from an  audience member here  of how would you describe the learning  culture and environment at the gsb  what's to describe the learning culture  and environment  i can go i think one of the things that  really appeals to me about the gsb  is just the collaborative culture i  think like it's not  cutthroat at all it's not like me  against you  like buying for like professors time or  for resources  it's like me and you working together to  uplift  both of us like uplift everyone like  what can i do to help you  to like improve your experience i think  it's all about uh  like growing and learning together as an  entire class as a and as an entire gsb  community and that's one of the things  that i love the most like i've always  felt really supported  um in terms of like emotional support  community support it just feels like a  family in a lot of ways  yeah i i would i would just add to that  when that jorge made  and people who have graduated more  recently let me know if this has changed  but  that there's a great non-disclosure  policy  which which invites what jorge said in a  very  obvious way right no one is kind of  competing when going to a  potential employer or whatever to say oh  i got this great or that great and so  that creates  a a very kind of collaborative culture  as what was saying  the only other thing i would say about i  think a culture that i as i reflect on  both my my experiences uh both at  harvard and stanford  i think the the goal that stanford has  of having all these kind of soft skill  classes  like interpersonal dynamics that nadu  was describing create a dynamic where  people are  constantly asking each other for  feedback  constantly role-playing things uh and  really trying to just  improve themselves as human beings that  i've never seen in another environment  so that that actually becomes a really  cool  uh culture where where it's  encouraged to to to reflect to give each  other feedback to be honest with each  other  um that i think was really unique at  least uh and what i've seen  so uh i really enjoy that as well  we have another question here of could  you elaborate on the extent  of teamwork and leadership opportunities  within the classes you took  i can uh go for that so there's actually  so many classes as people have already  mentioned like startup garage  there's also across the street classes  that bring in students from  all the different stanford schools to  work together so you could work  hands-on on various different projects  like i took an entrepreneurship class in  which we were  designing a business and i was working  with people across  gsb law school engineering school and  we essentially like came up with a  business plan came up with the  financial statements created a  presentation for  a pitch that we did to actual venture  capitalists from  sand hill road who gave us feedback on  our pitch  and in each of these experiences the  cool thing is that there's many  opportunities for you to take the lead  and figure out the dynamic with the team  that you're working on  so maybe in some cases you'll actually  want to be the one that takes the lead  on something  and you'll gain like leading  experience there but also in some cases  actually what's better for the team as a  whole  is for you to take a step back and to be  a follower or to be a supporting team  member rather than the leader in that  particular instance so i think that's  one of the  really cool learning experiences is that  you learn that leadership  doesn't just mean being the one at the  top who is managing  everything and telling people what to do  leadership means doing whatever is  necessary to make sure that your team is  successful  and that can mean working in a large  variety of roles which sometimes aren't  the one  that's out in the front having that very  visible role and it can mean being  a supporter or someone who is providing  a foundation  for the other team members to do their  best work  and you're able to learn about those  dynamics and see how you can fit in  most effectively in different situations  through the project-based classes  here's a question that was directed  towards sebastian but i think  anyone is also welcome to chime in here  which classes and or experiential  learning  experiences were the most useful for you  to really become a leader in your career  um i i think  a couple come to mind one is uh touchy  feely or  sorry interpersonal dynamics uh because  i think that gives you  a lot of feedback as new is saying on  how you come across  and what is the impact that certain  things that you hadn't even thought of  are having on others and so it's a very  unique setting in that front and that's  helped me  at least measure uh more carefully how i  have certain interactions from a  professional standpoint the other one is  the one i alluded to earlier the  managing difficult conversations  um which was one where you're basically  role-playing difficult conversations  firing someone having a big discussion  with a partner  about how you should split um  [Music]  equity uh those kind of things that you  know  are at a theoretical level very easy to  do but once you're actually  doing a role-playing exercise it at  least gives you one rep  uh to have done that before you're  actually doing it live and so i think  those two  for me at these were have been very  valuable in my career  um something i i'd add  uh that struck me from my time at the  gsb is that  i mean sometimes you hear this idea of  like ah did you really have to go to gsp  to learn about marketing  could you have bought that book same for  financing for accounting same for a lot  of things  um one thing that i really valued was  that you have  this combination of industry leaders  and uh world-class  researchers who have curated  all of these resources and what i found  very  impactful was how consistent  the the narrative the values the  worldviews the mindsets were  and lisa i don't know if this is  intentional or no  but uh pretty much everywhere the gsb  i heard this idea of like hey you know  to be a better leader you should listen  to be a better leader you should really  care for the other person  you should be kind you should live by  your values you should do this you  should that  i'm sorry um i think there's only one  class that offers  kind of like the dark side narrative  which is pass to power  i i didn't take it um but i my  understanding is that this professor's  narrative is  yeah all of that is beautiful  but you should not ignore how power  works in the world so this class  is focused on power but everything else  was this very consistent story like even  like how to fire people negotiations and  so on  everything was very consistent around  like hey just be a good person just just  bring your best and actually there's  hard evidence that supports that  this will help you be a better leader  better negotiator better sales person  better boss and so on  and that helped me just cut through all  the noise of so  many gurus and books out in the world  and i was like  okay amazing i can be myself i can be  trying to be a good person  and i can also be a better leader better  entrepreneur in doing so  and have a consistent theoretical base  and evidence-based behind  this uh and i found that super helpful  there's a lot on empathetic leadership  for sure that i that i've heard yeah  here's another um question from the  audience of how did you balance your  academic schedule with  all the other opportunities available at  the gsb and what is the typical  week in the life of a student look like  i see alana smiling do you want to take  that one  i'm laughing because you know i went  into consulting which is  famously a long hours profession but i  definitely have more free time now than  i did at the gsb  just because i scheduled everything so  tightly at the gsb right it wasn't that  i was doing  academics for 60 hours a week but i was  doing  something from 8 am to midnight every  day  and it's just a calendar management game  you quickly decide how much effort you  want to put into academics and people  vary a ton  on that right you want to put in  absolutely enough to get value  out of your classes but you can push  very far past that or you can do the  bare minimum to be learning something  and so that determines how many hours  you need to devote to academics  and the rest of it will fill up with  everything from coffee chats with people  to club events to  foam which is our tuesday night parties  and as long as you keep a good calendar  it's incredible how much you can fit  into a week  yeah i think to add on to that  um the framework that i often give  people is  first start with like what are you doing  all of this for  like if you have to pick three to five  priorities that you're going to use for  as a decision rubric for how you spend  your time  what are you doing all of this for and  having clarity on that i think  makes everything else much easier  because then you can say  okay this quarter this month this week  this day  i have 20 options which of those serve  that purpose  and then how am i going to mix them  together to  make this all work and the reality is  like  if you don't use calendars you're gonna  have a rough  two years um and i think  that you know you have to decide like  where do you need to double down and  spend time i took classes where i  learned a lot  but i didn't necessarily spend a lot of  hours  either because of my background or  because the content was structured such  that  i didn't have to do a lot of stuff  outside class  based on the course itself um  and so you can kind of mix and match  those right like it may not be a good  idea  in the quarter that you're trying to  plan a conference  and also recruit to take a bunch of  classes that require you to do  you know four hours of case reading and  writing  before each class so you kind of just  figure that out for yourself  um and the last thing i would say is um  think about kind of how things build on  each other  as you're determining your courses and  other experiences  so for example i wanted to take certain  classes before i went and did my  internship abroad  because i wanted to take some of those  perspectives  and go see what it looked like when i  was like  on the ground and so those are some  subtle things i would also just add to  think about  i see a question here um touching again  upon grade non-disclosure which was  brought up briefly beforehand and how it  aids to that collaborative  culture and learning environment at  stanford but this question is also a  really good one in that it talks about  how does that actually push you to take  some academic risks and to try some  classes that might be really hard and  outside of your comfort zone  can any of you share an example where  maybe you took a class that was  a little bit scary or intimidating or  outside your comfort zone  i was really scared to take financial  trading strategies which ended up being  one of my favorite classes  because it's a class where you spend a  bunch of your time in class  and a large portion of your grade is  determined by  how you do in these market simulations  where you are trading stocks or options  or whatever the topic of that class  period  is in real time in a sort of compressed  simulation of the market  and i came to the gsb from  zero finance background i had really  only taken my required  first year of gsb finance courses before  this  um so it was kind of a leap of faith to  take that class but it ended up being  one of the coolest classes i took  because it really taught me how the  market actually  works and what options  really are and what they need and how  people trade them beyond the  academic side of it that i had learned  in my foundation  finance class i think i have a similar  story to alana where  i also have no finance background coming  into the gsp  in fact at one of these advent  receptions i met a fellow classmate  who told me they were in pe and i asked  her if she was in physical education  because i had no idea that private  equity was a job  and i ended up taking like over seven  different finance classes at the gsb  most of them i took pass fail in the end  so corporate financial modeling  um the financial restructuring debt  class  these are all things i had absolutely  zero background in and  i think because of rate on disclosure  and generous pass fail policies at  the gsb i was able to take these classes  that i was interested in  knowing that i had no background in them  but wanted to learn more about it  and was able to i think now having  graduated have a decent amount of  finance knowledge relative to before  so great  this next question baby diego i'll have  you kick us off here um but what advice  would you have for um incoming mba  students who are really trying to  maximize  their academic experience and their  their classroom experience at the gsb  what advice would you give people  uh i i'll take the the  focus advice if someone else has the go  explore advice  go for it but i came in laser focus  right like my essay  was about lala it it talked about like  by name this is the thing i want to  build  so that helped me cut through a lot of  that noise of just  there's so many opportunities that you  could take  [Music]  it helped me say no to a lot of things  uh  really focus on uh like what classes to  take  every single project in every single  class somehow was  uh geared towards uh  this organization uh a lot of classmates  used me and lala to dip their toes  into social entrepreneurship into cyber  garage and so on so  that was beautiful and many of the  relationships that i ended up building  with professors with staff from the  center for social innovation  with classmates have all been hugely  valuable  yeah the others want to share advice on  how to maximize your academic experience  i think you should um i  i came in with not one thing but like  three things that i was really gearing  my  academics around one was developing  economies the other was interpersonal  development slash leadership  and then the third one was uh like  strategy and so i think for me  as i talk as i thought about what  classes to take they had to revolve  around that and you'll notice i didn't  say entrepreneurship  anywhere in there um which confuses  people because i managed to  get out of the gsb taking only one  entrepreneurship class  managing growing enterprises and  um i think that like a healthy amount of  uh curiosity is important but that looks  different for everyone and  my biggest piece of advice is like don't  try to replicate what alums did and  don't try to replicate what your  classmates are doing  because even if you do exactly what they  did you'll come out with a completely  different experience  anyway so you might as well live your  own experience and  i think that just takes some courage to  be like you know this is what i think i  want to do and whatever that looks like  just go with  it and the luxury is like every quarter  you can kind of change your mind um  but you only get six of them so  you know make use of them um and so  that's how i would think about it  great advice well i want to be cognizant  of our time so i want to thank  all of our panelists today for sharing  your perspectives  i also hope that this is just the start  of our attendees  mba journey and one way to continue that  is through  mystanford.mba if you haven't already  been to this site  definitely check it out you can create  your own personalized dashboard  that'll tee up classes or clubs that  might be of interest to you  student or alumni profiles that you  might relate to  if you start an application you can also  track your progress through that and  learn about other great events like  today so again thank you so much for  joining us  we really appreciate it and best of luck  on your mba journey  thank you  [Music]

